edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491923 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6dfe3b1d-64a2-5aa6-947a-b91c6e82fc88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491924 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6ec8b379-7fc5-5e9a-a35d-30cbe0d09a9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491933 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8197bfc5-0ad1-5f9c-bf1d-1334bb43fb45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491951 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a66ad25d-aec4-563a-8849-bb0750298a42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491994 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coefficient of ${s}^{5}$ in the expansion of  $(3+2s)^{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1561558e-21f5-576d-9b7c-91bcf7619901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491994 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=5,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{s}^{0},\\,{s}^{1},\\,{s}^{2},\\,{s}^{3},\\,{s}^{4},\\,{s}^{5}.\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${s}^{5}$ is the fifth. So, we are looking for the fifth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore, the fifth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1(3)^{0}(2s)^{5} & =32{s}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${s}^{5}$ in the expansion of $(3+2s)^{5}$ is $32.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492006 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(y+z)^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3050d934-f604-5f69-a586-6c218326013a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492006 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=y,$ and $b=z.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(y)^{4}(z)^{0}+4(y)^{3}(z)^{1}+6(y)^{2}(z)^{2}+4(y)^{1}(z)^{3}+1(y)^{0}(z)^{4} \\\\  & =1({y}^{4})(1)+4({y}^{3})(z)+6({y}^{2})({z}^{2})+4(y)({z}^{3})+1(1)({z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+4y{z}^{3}+{z}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492016 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{4}$ in the expansion of  ${(z+2x)}^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "583748e6-2c8b-5634-bc36-5084c91f8d81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492016 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{z}^{4}{x}^{0},\\,{z}^{3}{x}^{1},\\,{z}^{2}{x}^{2},\\,{z}^{1}{x}^{3},\\,{z}^{0}{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${x}^{4}$ is the fifth. So, we are looking for the fifth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the fifth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1{(z)}^{0}{(2x)}^{4}=1(1)(16{x}^{4})=16{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{4}$ in the expansion of ${(z+2x)}^{4}$ is $16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492024 #edb/ref 1071 "Express the missing number as the sum of two numbers from Pascal's triangle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & ◻ &  & 35 &  & 35 &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f10ffe0-480f-5e92-a4dc-edbf0f536c4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492024 #edb/ref 1074 "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $6$ and $15.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & ◻ &  & 35 &  & 35 &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $6$ and $15.$ \n\n$$\n◻=6+15\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492029 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the expansion of  $(2x-3)^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7cdcec28-9eea-5d7b-863d-1e35767ec2c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492029 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{x}^{4},\\,{x}^{3},\\,{x}^{2},\\,{x}^{1},\\,{x}^{0}\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${x}^{2}$ is the third. So, we are looking for the third term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the third term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(2x)^{2}{(-3)}^{2} & =6(4{x}^{2})(9)=216{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the expansion of $(2x-3)^{4}$ is $216.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492045 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{(r+m)}^{3}=A{r}^{3}+B{r}^{2}m+Cr{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab2cb57c-ba4d-5126-ab49-971b151088ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492045 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=r,$ and $b=m.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r+m)^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(r)^{3}(m)^{0}+3(r)^{2}(m)^{1}+3(r)^{1}(m)^{2}+1(r)^{0}(m)^{3} \\\\  & =1({r}^{3})(1)+3({r}^{2})(m)+3(r)({m}^{2})+1(1)({m}^{3}) \\\\  & ={r}^{3}+3{r}^{2}m+3r{m}^{2}+{m}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=1,B=3,$ and $C=3.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=1+3+3=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492046 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac49f7f6-9f77-5cda-ac3b-cdee9bcbb73f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492046 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}},$ and $b=-{z}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{3}{(-{z}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{2}{(-{z}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{1}{(-{z}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})}^{0}{(-{z}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}})(1)+3(\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{z}^{4}})(-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}})({z}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{{z}^{4}}+\\frac{6y{z}^{4}}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+6y{z}^{2}-{z}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the binomial expansion in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2y}{{z}^{2}}-{z}^{2})}^{3}=-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{{z}^{6}}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$ and descending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n-{z}^{6}+6y{z}^{2}-\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492051 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coefficient of ${t}^{9}$ in the expansion of  ${(4-2{t}^{3})}^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb756db8-08d4-574e-9721-71fce97c078b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492051 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=4,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{({t}^{3})}^{0},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{1},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{2},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{3},\\,{({t}^{3})}^{4}\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns ${t}^{9}$ is the fourth. So, we are looking for the fourth term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore, the fourth term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({4}^{1}){(-2{t}^{3})}^{3} & =4(4)(-8{t}^{9})=-128{t}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${t}^{9}$ in the expansion of ${(4-2{t}^{3})}^{4}$ is $-128.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492054 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in descending powers of $t,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(t+2)^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb084350-7fbe-5022-a4f9-36da32efbecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492054 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=t,$ and $b=2.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t+2)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}{b}^{0}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(t)^{4}(2)^{0}+4(t)^{3}(2)^{1}+6(t)^{2}(2)^{2}+4(t)^{1}(2)^{3}+1(t)^{0}(2)^{4} \\\\  & =1({t}^{4})(1)+4({t}^{3})(2)+6({t}^{2})({2}^{2})+4(t)({2}^{3})+1({t}^{0})({2}^{4}) \\\\  & ={t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}+32t+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $t,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion are\n\n$$\n{t}^{4}+8{t}^{3}+24{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492069 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492069 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & 21 &  & 35 &  & ◻ &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eda5d85b-de52-5d2f-abad-d370d89d2617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492069 #edb/ref 1074 "In Pascal's triangle, the first and last number in each row is $1.$ The other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are $20$ and $15.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 6 &  & 15 &  & 20 &  & 15 &  & 6 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 7 &  & 21 &  & 35 &  & ◻ &  & 21 &  & 7 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $20$ and $15.$ \n\n$$\n◻=20+15=35\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
